SM(587,{"n":"Hebrews Introduction Part 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews Introduction Part 1.mp3","d":2611.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.72,"Tonight we are beginning St."],[27.68,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[29.36,"I'll go ahead and say it."],[32.88,"So most of most of tonight's gonna be introduction, especially because this is Hebrews, and there's uh sort of a lot to talk about uh in terms of this book."],[46.48,"Um because as I just implied, I refer to it as Saint Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, but uh there's a whole lot of people, including a bunch of church fathers who don't think St."],[58.32,"Paul wrote it."],[59.28,"Uh it's kind of not really an epistle, and we're not even sure it was written per se to a group of Hebrews, but more on each of those later."],[72.44,"It's sort of like the Holy Roman Empire thing in Voltaire."],[75.56,"Um but uh we'll be we'll be discussing that uh tonight here as we uh introduce."],[85.16,"So we'll start with the Saint Paul part."],[88.92,"That's so uh the epistle has always been known as Saint Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, uh even by people who didn't think St."],[99.64,"Paul wrote it."],[100.76,"Uh it was just firmly established very early on."],[105.68,"To give you an idea of how early on, we've talked before about how when we're talking about what an original text would be for the New Testament, now that's a thing that doesn't actually really exist."],[141.28,"Paul's epistles for today."],[144.12,"Paul's epistles, Saint Paul dictated them."],[148.04,"He didn't write them by hand."],[150.06,"We know that because we have those places in different epistles where he says, See, I am writing with my own hand."],[156.44,"You don't, you know, and in a couple places, see with what large letters."],[160.37,"So clearly indicating that's a change from the rest of the letter we've just been reading that was being written by someone else."],[167.17,"Um, and that was done by a sort of secretary that was called an emanuensis uh in Greek."],[174.93,"And so uh the way it worked is that the person dictating the letter would dictate it."],[184.13,"The secretary or the scribe would write down what was said, but would also, you know, correct grammar, put it in good polished literary form."],[194.59,"Because spoken and written in any language are a little different in terms of how you do it."],[199.87,"Polish it up a little, make it nice."],[202.24,"And then once they were done writing it, they would give it back to the person who dictated it to go over it."],[208.42,"And look at all those little changes and stuff and make any corrections they wanted to make."],[213.46,"And then the person who was the scribe would then take the letter to the recipient and read it aloud."],[219.96,"Wherever it was, and be expected to be able to answer questions about it."],[225.96,"So that's how like an epistle is written."],[228.93,"One of the individual epistles."],[230.93,"But we also talked about how, in some cases, like 2 Cor Corintinthihiansans, the, that's actually what we now call 2 actually has contents of two, at least two of St."],[244.25,"Paul's epistles kind of edited together, into it."],[248.51,"St. Paul wrote between at least four and maybe six epistles to the church in Corinth."],[255.87,"And we have the contents of at least two of them put together in 2 Corinthians."],[262.03,"And you can kind of see the breaks."],[267.19,"But then beyond that, uh, we talked about how uh the uh the books of the Bible did not circulate individually for very long."],[277.35,"They of course did at first."],[278.89,"When they were first written."],[280.25,"Saint Paul wrote the epistles at different times."],[281.01,"The gospels were written at different times by different authors"],[286.47,"Um the uh general epistles that we're about to get into, you know, James versus 2 Peter, Jude, first, 2nd, 3rd John, likewise, Revelation, likewise."],[297.11,"Um, but very quickly they started to be gathered together into collections."],[302.55,"And the first one of those was actually Saint Paul's epistles."],[307.19,"We know that they were all gathered together in one place by 100 AD."],[312.87,"Um, but um it may that may have started happening even earlier."],[320.92,"Evidence of that is, for example, in 2 Peter, where St."],[328.4,"Peter refers to St."],[330.0,"Paul's epistles. He says St."],[332.09,"Paul wrote to you, the people he's writing to, but then he also refers to St."],[336.25,"Paul's epistles plural."],[339.39,"Meaning whoever he was writing to would have been familiar with more than one of them, meaning they had to be kind of circulating."],[346.89,"So they're probably already starting to be gathered together for circulation."],[350.97,"But they were all collected by AD 100."],[355.53,"And all of the manuscripts we currently possess of any of St."],[361.21,"Paul's epistles, manuscripts meaning handwritten copies, all of the copies we currently have, going all the way back, are all copies of collections of St."],[368.89,"Paul's epistles We don't have a single manuscript that's from an individual epistle that was circulating by itself."],[378.93,"So by 100 AD, there's they're circulating as a collection."],[383.01,"By the time in the second century where we have any manuscripts, they're only circulating as a collection."],[389.97,"Um the earliest one we have of St."],[392.85,"Paul's epistles is uh called P46."],[397.49,"The P is for papyrus."],[401.11,"Most of our later manuscripts are on Vellum, which is Animal Skin."],[406.91,"Uh, but uh the earliest ones we have are on papyrus, which is primitive paper."],[416.87,"It's bashed out reads."],[418.57,"To make make what's basically paper."],[421.77,"And those are mostly found in Egypt because and then a few in in parts of the Judean desert."],[429.93,"If you're taking account the Dead Sea Scrolls, for example."],[432.97,"And that's because the climate is so dry that papyrus like that could be preserved for this long."],[441.37,"Because as you can imagine, anything paper related is not going to last 2,000 years, uh, it's gonna is gonna rot if there's any moisture at all, as we well know in Louisiana."],[453.13,"So um, but if you're in the Egyptian desert, there's no moisture. and so you're able to preserve paper much longer"],[461.65,"So the P it's P46 because it's on uh papyrus, and it is the 46th papyrus biblical manuscript that they found."],[473.33,"They just number them in the order that they find them."],[477.81,"Um and so P46 is a manuscript of St."],[484.53,"Paul's epistles. Um it's mostly complete in the sense that we have parts of all the books, it is not mostly complete in the sense that there aren't holes in the pages and that kind of thing, which you always get with parchment manuscripts, but we have identifiable fragments of, so we can. tell what the contents are"],[504.81,"And we can tell that it dates to the second half of the second century."],[510.57,"So somewhere between 150 and 200."],[513.85,"That's the earliest manuscript of St."],[516.81,"Paul's Epistles we have, P46."],[519.45,"And it includes St."],[521.29,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[544.41,"Because they're in order by length, like we've talked about. and it is the second longest after uh after Romans"],[553.57,"So whoever, whatever scribe copied P46, may have been copying the order from even earlier."],[561.65,"But whoever originally gathered them and put them in that order was treating Hebrews just like the rest of St."],[569.01,"Paul's epistles. That person did not see any reason why it was different than the others or should be in a different in a different collection."],[578.45,"We also have from around 150 AD, the general epistles."],[586.57,"James, first second Peter, versus 2nd, 3 John, Jude, start circulating together."],[590.97,"The general or Catholic epistles start circulating together in collections."],[592.25,"Hebrews is never in it"],[598.83,"So the testimony of the manuscripts is that the scribes who were copying these things and passing them down thought it belonged in the St."],[607.39,"Paul collection, not in the other apostolic writings collection."],[612.91,"When there were two."],[622.15,"St. Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[623.67,"That's why it's going to be titled that."],[625.83,"It's also important to know that Hebrews was primarily being read authoritatively in churches."],[637.11,"I'll digress on this a little bit."],[638.95,"Okay. So we're used to hearing this kind of misrepresentation of how canon works."],[652.44,"How they decided which books were quote unquote in the Bible."],[659.64,"And it's a misrepresentation in a ton of ways."],[663.66,"Like nobody ever voted."],[665.82,"They never got together and voted."],[667.66,"They didn't say anything about the New Testament canon at the Council of Nicaea at all."],[672.66,"Um, there's a whole bunch of misnomers floating around about that that people think."],[677.06,"Um, but the the biggest misnomer is this idea that people were like looking at different books and trying to decide if they were canonical or not."],[689.03,"Canon, what it means for a work to be canonical, and this is used outside the context of the Bible."],[696.08,"People will talk about the Western canon of literature."],[699.18,"Um there's a uh the term British canon is actually used to refer to music, like mostly pop music of the mid to late 20th century."],[711.95,"Um, so this is used and so canon just refers to works that exercise a sort of authority and prominence of place within a given culture or community."],[723.39,"So you could use it in all kinds of things"],[727.48,"So when we're talking about the canon for the church, we're talking about these early Christian communities that grew up in these different places of the Roman world and even outside the Roman world in Persia and other places."],[740.28,"Um they had texts that they were reading liturgically."],[747.29,"From from the beginning."],[749.74,"So when St."],[751.02,"Paul's going into synagogues."],[752.8,"The'yre opening up the scrolls and they're reading right from the Hebrew scriptures, and he's preaching from them and he's preaching about Christ."],[759.66,"And so we see already by I'll reference Second Peter again, St."],[764.46,"Peter lumps in St."],[765.42,"Paul's. epistles with quote unquote the other scriptures"],[770.43,"So even by that time, so we're talking about the mid 60s AD."],[777.37,"The first century, it was seen that these writings by the apostles were of a similar authority and were exercising that kind of authority in these communities."],[786.73,"So any given community had texts that they were reading in their liturgical services and that context to be a member of one of those communities and say, is this book canonical?"],[805.93,"Because, well, do we read it in the services?"],[810.49,"Yes. Well, then, yeah."],[813.45,"The answer is no, then, well, not for us."],[818.22,"Um, and Christianity inherited from the various forms of Judaism, the different Judaisms that are going around at the at the time."],[828.64,"The different forms of Judaism all had different canons."],[831.38,"They all had different books they were reading."],[833.48,"Qumran, uh, in terms of number of copies we found of books in the Dead Sea Scrolls at Qumran."],[840.44,"So this is a non-Christian Jewish group."],[843.05,"That that ended its life in the first century AD."],[846.65,"Uh in number of copies, so these would be the books that they were using the the most most, because they had copies."],[850.97,"Number two, the Book of Enoch or First Enoch"],[853.25,"Number one, Genesis."],[857.41,"Number three, Jubilees."],[859.25,"Number four, Exodus."],[862.69,"So. So they had a very different concept of what books were authoritative than the Pharisees did."],[871.11,"And who, you know, as we may know from other stuff we talked about in the New Testament, their view of what books were authoritative was very different than the Sadducees, who only accepted the Torah, as authoritative."],[881.45,"And you go to Alexandria, Egypt, and they had for second, third Maccabees, that they were using."],[888.07,"You know, and so, and you go to the Ethiopian Jews at that time, and they had this huge volume of literature."],[895.75,"So that was true of these Jewish communities."],[897.83,"And so the uh the Christian communities that rose out of those Jewish communities and then had other non-Jews join them, took over those, whatever they got from the Jewish community as their old testament for the most part."],[915.59,"They just sort of said, oh, and then they added in the apostolic writings as their new testament."],[920.87,"Uh, but there was never among those different Judaisms, they didn't sit around and argue about which books were canonical."],[927.91,"They didn't sit around and go, we don't even see the Pharisees and Sadducees arguing about that."],[931.75,"They argue about other stuff."],[932.75,"They argue about the resurrection, they argue about you know theology, and how to interpret the law."],[938.69,"The dony't argue about what books are canonical."],[939.69,"s. That' not right"],[942.74,"So you didn't have anybody going to the Ethiopian Jews, like, why are you reading this weird stuff?"],[947.14,"It was just like, oh, well, that's their way."],[950.4,"And so the the churches basically do the same thing."],[953.2,"Like they're not sitting there like going out and condemning."],[957.25,"What you see is not."],[960.02,"We've got these right Gnostic gospels, Gnostic writings, other non-canonical, other heretical, literature floating around."],[970.2,"And what you see is those communities, starting with like St."],[974.2,"Irenaeus and Against Heresies in the second century, he doesn't come out and say and just go after the books."],[981.24,"the books They're reading wrong."],[981.72,"He goes after what they're teaching"],[987.32,"He says, this community is not descended from the apostles."],[989.74,"They're teaching this, that's not what the apostles taught."],[992.06,"They're teaching this, that's not what the apostles taught."],[994.06,"They're teaching this, that's not what the apostles taught."],[996.96,"And then he may add at the end, yeah, they're getting it from these goofy books."],[1001.73,"But none of these people are sola scriptura, and so none of these communities are based on the books they're reading."],[1011.07,"The the books generally are a function of what they believe."],[1015.24,"So if you if you take a copy of the Gospel of Truth or the Gospel of Thomas in the in the third early third century, and you walk into a Christian community gathering with it and show it to them, they're going to read it, they're going to say, that's not what we believe."],[1035.02,"That's not what we teach."],[1036.14,"So Gary, so they were they were judging the books by what they received, not vice versa."],[1042.32,"They weren't judging what we should be teaching based on the books."],[1045.78,"Because they had this living memory going back to the apostles of what was being taught and what the gospel was and who Christ was."],[1057.9,"There were not these debates like we're gonna excommunicate you if you don't accept for second Peter."],[1065.18,"Like what happened, it happened sort of organically."],[1068.48,"These communities, as they grow and and during times when persecution abated, they would start to make contacts with each other."],[1075.81,"And so they would see similarities and differences."],[1079.03,"And they'd be exposed to different books."],[1080.71,"So there are communities that didn't have second and third John."],[1083.59,"They didn't think second and third John were heretical and they threw them out."],[1086.47,"They never saw them before."],[1088.79,"And so they encountered another community that had them."],[1091.93,"And so then there was a process, a part of the getting doing process is they would then adopt."],[1096.27,"Second and third job."],[1097.31,"So that happens over a gradual process."],[1100.43,"And at any given point in time, what's canonical and what isn't is a very objective thing."],[1106.64,"Because a t a text is either I'm sorry?"],[1108.72,"You mean subject"],[1109.88,"No, objective. You could walk into any of those communities, and there are certain texts that are being treated as authoritative."],[1116.28,"That's the canon."],[1118.21,"You don't need a table of contents, you don't need a list, you don't need a."],[1122.1,"Um, and so for example, the first time we get an actual canon list for the New Testament that matches our current New Testament is St."],[1133.38,"Athanasius near the end of the fourth century."],[1137.52,"In 387, he sends out his pa in his PASCO letter."],[1140.96,"But even that is not like here I am as Patriarch of Alexandria, laying out the authoritative, because first of all, he couldn't do that for the whole church."],[1151.04,"But um, because one bishop can't do that."],[1154.04,"Sorry, Rome. Uh, but also, he uh he isn't even saying these are the these are the books that are inspired by God, this is the can't he says, I don't want any books other than these being read in my churches."],[1172.46,"So if there was some church out somewhere or some monastery out in the desert in Egypt that wasn't reading Second Peter, he wasn't like, you need to add Second Peter to your lectionary right now, or you're a hair."],[1184.58,"He's saying, if you're reading something else, if you're reading the Gospel of Thomas, if you're reading the Gospel of Judith, if you're reading it, I'm telling you to stop, reading those things."],[1194.4,"On thely stuff on this list should you be reading."],[1194.84,"And it doesn't have an old testament list"],[1198.96,"It's just, it's just New Testament books."],[1202.29,"So canon forms it a very different way."],[1205.59,"And I say all that here in this context because Hebrews is pretty much unknown in the West until the fourth century."],[1217.51,"And it's not really fully embraced."],[1219.91,"And what I mean by fully embraced is where people start quoting it authoritatively and commenting on it in the writings of the fathers until the beginning of the 5th century."],[1229.11,"It's mostly St."],[1229.83,"Augustine and St."],[1230.79,"Jerome who start using it."],[1236.95,"And this is a digression I won't go down right now, but I'll just seed it here."],[1244.11,"That's why you don't have anyone talking about the atonement in the West before."],[1250.67,"Because as we're going to see, a big chunk of Hebrews is talking about Christ and the Day of Atonement."],[1256.03,"You don't see anything about atonement going on in the West during that period."],[1260.27,"But the East, the Eastern churches, start talking about Hebrews right from the beginning."],[1267.07,"You see it being quoted authoritatively, you see it being commented on, you see it showing up all over the place and just being treated as a writing of St."],[1274.59,"Paul. So and so there are some books of the New Testament, like the four Gospelsp.els by 150 were universally accepted as these are the Gos and just these four"],[1291.19,"Which is kind of amazing."],[1292.55,"I don't want to digress too much on this, but it's it's kind of amazing."],[1297.99,"Not that they excluded the other stuff, because if you read Gnostic Gospels, they're very different."],[1303.13,"Uh people say John's different than the synoptics, but compared to like the Gospel of Thomas, they're identical."],[1310.49,"Um but interesting that it's four."],[1315.21,"That we don't see, we have no evidence of major Christian churches just using one of the four."],[1325.46,"Like, like you'd think maybe this this region region would be would. be using Matthew, and would be using Mark, and this one losing using Luke"],[1332.54,"And then, sort of over time, as they got to know each other, they'd all start using each other's, but that's not how it happens at all."],[1337.98,"The four of them are there in tandem."],[1341.44,"All together, like from the beginning."],[1344.0,"It's kind of an interesting and amazing phenomenon."],[1346.72,"Um, but again, it's a digression."],[1348.96,"We're talking about Hebrews."],[1350.16,"So, but but um so there, but there are some New Testament books like that that just get accepted universally very early throughout throughout the Christian church."],[1359.76,"There are other texts like Revelation that it takes forever."],[1364.72,"Revelation wasn't really functioning canonically in the whole Western church until the 15th.th century, and the Eastern churches until the 6 century"],[1378.04,"And Hebrews is kind of that way in the West, but not the East."],[1381.96,"The East was more familiar with it."],[1385.56,"There are all kinds of theories as to why that might be, all of which I think are mostly conjecture."],[1392.68,"Like you get a lot of, well, there's still people there speaking Semitic languages, and you know."],[1398.92,"But it's the kind of thing you can never prove, and everybody can guess, and maybe, who knows."],[1406.28,"That's just sort of how it historically happened."],[1408.52,"Okay So all that is to say, in the Eastern churches from the very beginning, Hebrews is accepted as. as part of this Pauline corpus, part of this literary unit St"],[1424.4,"Paul's epistles. But that isn't to say that the people who were reading it didn't notice some things."],[1436.64,"Some of it we're going to talk about as we move on into the introduction, like the fact that is it really an epistle?"],[1442.48,"But also just the Greek and the Greek style and everything is very different from what we see in St."],[1452.64,"Paul's Epistles. And I'm not talking about different epistemists"],[1456.96,"We talked about when we started the pastoral epistles, and also when we introduced like Ephesians and Colossians, these epistles of St."],[1467.16,"Paul that like modern scholars doubt St."],[1470.68,"Paul wrote. We're not talking about those kind of minor differences where we had explanations."],[1476.84,"We had kind of ready explanations for that."],[1480.76,"Reasons why, for example, for example, writing a letter to a person and writing a letter to a community, why that would look different."],[1487.54,"There's obvious reasons why he'd be talking about different themes to different audiences."],[1492.5,"We're not talking about those kind of differences."],[1494.34,"We're talking about uh the way that if you if you take St."],[1498.58,"Paul's epistle to the Romans and you take St."],[1500.74,"Mark's Gospel, which is written in most it biblical introductions try to be nice to St."],[1506.9,"Which it's terrible"],[1508.62,"Mark, and they refer to his Greek as like rustic."],[1513.75,"It's terrible Greek."],[1515.05,"He's communicating powerful things, but his Greek style is bad."],[1519.7,"He's not a he's not great."],[1521.62,"And especially if you contrast him to somebody like St."],[1524.02,"Luke or St."],[1524.82,"Paul, where it's like really polished."],[1527.62,"Like St. Paul writes the way Epictetus writes, like in his Greek style and stuff."],[1534.18,"Um it's impressive and complicated."],[1537.72,"Whereas St. Mark is just a big line of part of symbols."],[1541.24,"Um, but I won't digress too much more about that."],[1545.0,"My old bone to pick with St."],[1546.92,"Mark. But um, I love St."],[1549.24,"Mark's gospel. I hate trying to read it in Greek."],[1552.16,"The um so it it's like that."],[1557.68,"Like comparing comparing St."],[1559.28,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews to St."],[1560.72,"Paul's epistle to the Romans, they do not look like they were written by the same person."],[1567.17,"And it's again beyond the level of just like, well, this is a different scribe."],[1572.98,"Um, but that said, that said, a lot of the concepts, the ideas, uh, the way that St."],[1583.22,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews uses Old Testament quotes and the way it interprets and applies them, um, just general concept and terms of phrasing, that kind of thing, all looks very much like how St."],[1593.46,"Paul did things"],[1600.15,"Uh, and so this is a case where we're not just talking about modern critical scholars."],[1606.63,"From the beginning, church fathers who are reading this."],[1610.07,"Um, and you especially see this, because it sort of shows up a little later in the West than in the East."],[1616.72,"A lot of the Western fathers, when they're first experiencing it, they're used to reading St."],[1621.6,"Paul's epistles, and now they have this other thing coming in, and they're not rejecting it, but they're kind of going, what's up?"],[1628.18,"What's what's happening here?"],[1629.7,"And so you get kind of you get it's really one set of theories, but there's kind of two variations within it."],[1636.82,"Uh the overall set of theories is that okay, uh somebody else other than St."],[1646.3,"Paul. St. Paul is involved in this somehow, but someone else other than St."],[1649.74,"Paul is also involved."],[1651.42,"We'll call that the overarching thing."],[1654.84,"So how that plays out in different theories, and right now I'm still just talking about church fathers, um, is either uh some close affiliate of St."],[1669.56,"Paul. We've seen as we've gone through St."],[1671.94,"Paul's epistles. He has a lot of traveling companions."],[1676.29,"He has, and he's had co-authors on some of his epistles, especially St."],[1679.97,"Timothy. Has written some of his epistles with them"],[1682.79,"Um that one of those folks wrote it."],[1689.91,"And since one of those one of those folks wrote it, but since they're a close associate of St."],[1694.47,"Paul, they have the same theological outlook as Saint Paul."],[1699.19,"They've been learning from St."],[1700.71,"Paul. They've been being mentored by St."],[1702.79,"Paul, like we saw with Saints Timothy and Titus."],[1706.41,"And so they kind of think and express themselves the way he did, but they have a different writing style."],[1713.55,"And so Saint Paul is involved, but not directly involved."],[1718.99,"The other variation of that is that St"],[1724.63,"Paul is involved in producing something."],[1729.88,"And then one of those associates turns that something into Hebrews."],[1739.4,"So, for example, there are some fathers who propose, and this theory has a long life."],[1749.0,"Thomas Aquinas still thought so."],[1752.2,"This is where he went with it."],[1754.12,"So that gets you know all the way to twelve hundred."],[1757.36,"Um that Saint Paul wrote something in Hebrew or Aramaic."],[1766.6,"And because it was directed to Jewish folks."],[1771.93,"Wrote something in Hebrew and Aramaic, and Saint Luke translated it."],[1777.85,"And Saint Luke's Greek translation is what we now call Hebrews."],[1782.33,"The reason Saint Luke is the one who usually gets singled out for that in these theories is that there are a lot of places in Hebrews where the Greek looks like Saint Luke's."],[1798.13,"If it wasn't Saint Luke, that's odd."],[1801.75,"The reason that's odd is Saint Luke, what we have from him is Gospel according to St."],[1809.59,"Luke, Acts of the Apostles."],[1811.69,"Both of which are narrative."],[1815.23,"This is the story of Christ's life, the uh the um, then the apostles, especially focusing in the latter part of the Acts of the Apostles on St."],[1825.39,"Paul. So it's like historical narrative."],[1828.75,"And Saint Luke sets it up by saying he did all this research and compiled all these sources."],[1833.87,"And in keeping with the form he's using, uh, Saint Luke is very clearly aping, like, especially Thucydides, in his style in those books."],[1845.87,"He's deliberately writing in a Greek historical style."],[1850.97,"So by using that style, he's putting his literary works into that genre of history, that he's presenting."],[1855.61,"Which of course isn't modern history, but ancient history"],[1861.5,"But that's how he's putting it forward."],[1863.82,"Okay. So St."],[1866.06,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, we're gonna talk about whether it's an epistle, but no matter how you slice it, it's not historical narrative."],[1873.4,"Like this is not a historical narrative at all."],[1876.52,"Uh so the fact that there are these connections to St."],[1879.88,"Luke's style are weird based on it being in another genre, if it's not the same author."],[1888.76,"So that I think is the best evidence for St."],[1892.44,"Luke being involved."],[1896.38,"And uh to put my cards on the table, by the end of this introduction, I'm going to put forward my personal opinion."],[1906.5,"And I'm just saying it's opinion."],[1908.98,"Because there's definitely not a single teaching of the church on this."],[1914.18,"The fathers are all over the place."],[1916.1,"And later authors of the church as well."],[1920.1,"But I'm going to be putting forward a version of that."],[1922.82,"I do think St."],[1923.7,"Luke is involved."],[1925.06,"And I think for that reason, because the stylistic things are it's too strong a connection for something from another genre."],[1935.12,"To not have him involved."],[1936.72,"And he also is, of course, closely associated with St."],[1939.68,"Paul and traveling with him."],[1941.04,"But I'll come back to that."],[1942.56,"So, in terms of the other theories."],[1944.89,"And the other theories are basically versions of some other affiliate of St."],[1949.93,"Paul wrote it."],[1951.53,"And you will find people, both church fathers and modern people in modern school, going to bat for whole bunches of these."],[1979.73,"uh he's also not heretical. But"],[1981.41,"So but he suggested that the Theotokos wrote it."],[1985.25,"Um I haven't encountered anyone. else in the history of the church defending that idea, but it's intriguing"],[1993.73,"What was the name of it all?"],[1995.41,"I'm gonna have to I would have to check again."],[1997.73,"I think he's a pseudo on top of everything else."],[2000.93,"Like, not that he wrote under another name, but they thought it was a work of somebody, and it turns out it's not, so he gets labeled as pseudo."],[2008.37,"It might be one of the pseudo-Augustans."],[2011.73,"Um, but I will double check that."],[2014.13,"Um yeah, who but that that's the weirdest one."],[2018.69,"But the more common ones you get, um, one of the first ones is Tertullian."],[2024.61,"Uh, and this is Tertullian while he was still a Christian."],[2027.97,"Uh uh thought it was written by Barnabas."],[2032.69,"And Saint Barnabas has been a popular pick in church history"],[2040.33,"Uh, going all the way up to Martin Luther, but even contemporary scholars want to peg it as Saint Barnabas."],[2046.97,"The problem with that is Tertullian's argument, the core of Tertullian's argument, the reason he picks Barnabas out of all of St."],[2053.37,"Paul's associates, he picks St."],[2055.05,"Barnabas because he says, Well, look how similar the Greek is to the Epistle of Barnabas."],[2061.69,"It's like, okay, but now we're presupposing that St."],[2065.77,"Barnabas really wrote the Epistle of Barnabas."],[2068.86,"Which I I don't know that we think that necessarily."],[2073.5,"Um, so that's kind of a problematic argument."],[2076.7,"Um, and and it part of the problem with these theories is, you know, you can you can name an associate of St."],[2084.26,"Paul, and it's like, well, okay, if you think an associate of St."],[2088.34,"Paul wrote it, maybe it was that one."],[2092.64,"But it's hard to come up with any very strong argument that it's this particular associate of St."],[2100.32,"Paul as opposed to all the others."],[2102.22,"So, like, the only thing you really have for St."],[2103.82,"Barnabas, we don't have anything St."],[2105.58,"Barnabas wrote in the New Testament."],[2108.24,"So you could say, well, the Epistle of Barnabas, the Epistle of Barnabas is a little problematic in a couple of places."],[2113.68,"That's why it's not in the New Testament."],[2115.68,"It never held authority like that in Christian communities."],[2121.72,"So that's not a strong one."],[2123.08,"And we're gonna see that with a bunch of these."],[2124.52,"So there's also people who say that they think uh Saint Apollos wrote it."],[2129.0,"Well, okay. Like, I mean, but we don't have anything written by Saint Apollos to compare it to."],[2137.7,"Why him as opposed to anyone else?"],[2140.26,"Um, but he's a very popular one."],[2143.86,"Um now recently, uh this is this is where I get to bash our 19th century German friends, and especially the king of 19th century German uh New Testament scholarship, Adolf von Harnack."],[2162.78,"That's just has one of the most German. names ever"],[2167.98,"He put forward this theory, which as soon as I tell you, you're gonna know why all of a sudden this is popular today."],[2178.06,"That it was written by St."],[2179.9,"Priscilla of Priscilla and Aquila."],[2185.82,"Uh so here's the argument."],[2190.14,"I'm gonna try and steel man this."],[2192.22,"I'm gonna try and give you the best version."],[2195.34,"Um so um we're gonna talk in a minute about where it was written to."],[2202.62,"Uh and but so we'll go into more detail on this."],[2205.02,"But there's a there's a general consensus that this was written to or for uh the Jewish community, the Judean community in Rome."],[2217.3,"Okay. When we meet Saints Priscilla and Aquila, who are numbered among the 70, and are saints."],[2223.3,"So I'm not saying anything negative about them."],[2225.54,"I want to make that clear, because I'm gonna be picking on Von Harnak, and I'm gonna be picking on this idea, but I'm not saying anything negative about the saints."],[2235.1,"Um when we meet Saints Priscilla and Aquila in the Acts of the Apostles, they had it was when they had been expelled from Rome under Claudius, and had gone to Corinth, and that's where St."],[2250.38,"Paul meets them, and they they become Christians."],[2255.14,"And we've we saw them pop up later in the epistles that it was in their home that some of the saints were meeting."],[2262.42,"So they become important, and in church tradition, uh St."],[2265.94,"Aquila becomes a bishop, uh, and they're numbered among the 70."],[2270.1,"Uh so the thing that people have lit on, especially in recent days, however, is that at least at a few points where the two of them are mentioned, Saint Priscilla is mentioned first."],[2282.26,"She being the wife."],[2286.26,"And so that is most likely because she was from some kind of prominent family."],[2293.24,"That that she would be mentioned first."],[2295.4,"The money probably came to her."],[2297.74,"If they're hosting the church. and if they're living in Rome and able to move this kind of thing"],[2302.34,"This this is these are wealthy people."],[2304.84,"And so her being mentioned first probably means that there was some inheritance going on."],[2309.62,"And under the Torah, if you didn't have any sons, uh the oldest daughter could inherit."],[2314.43,"There's in the book of Numbers, there's a dispute that Moses settles and establishes that."],[2320.11,"Um so that's probably why."],[2323.92,"But you could see how someone like von Harnack and and recent people would parlay that into she's listed first."],[2333.94,"She's this prominent member of the community."],[2336.48,"They came from that Jewish community in Rome."],[2339.28,"So if this is written for them, they could have written it."],[2340.8,"And so I bet she wrote it"],[2345.7,"And so now if you uh I checked today."],[2350.5,"I was just curious."],[2351.7,"If you put into Google who wrote Hebrews."],[2354.74,"I was super curious, just what would come up."],[2356.83,"Whether it would just be like nobody knows."],[2359.55,"Umrigin, for example, said, well, it's St."],[2364.59,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, because that's how he received it."],[2367.39,"He said, but who actually wrote it, God knows."],[2372.19,"Um but uh so I just I what what what'll I get?"],[2379.07,"And uh we'll just say a prominent portion of what I got was a woman. wrote one of the books of the New Testament"],[2389.43,"Not a 19th century German guy had a theory that a woman wrote one of the books of the New Testament, not maybe not is a just straight out saying, a woman wrote one of the books of the New Testament, the church has been lying about it for 2,000 years and trying to say St."],[2406.33,"Paul wrote it right."],[2407.29,"All of that, all of that nonsense."],[2412.17,"Okay. Um so I gave you that this really go do your own research."],[2421.69,"I didn't just straw man this, I gave you all the evidence."],[2425.69,"All the evidence is Saints Priscilla and Aquila are from the community. that most people agree this was written for"],[2435.01,"And the reason for choosing her as opposed to her husband as the author is nothing."],[2441.94,"Even if you were persuaded that one of them wrote it, there's no particular evidence for either one."],[2449.43,"Um, so there's just and going against it, there's one point near the end of the Epistle to the Hebrews where the author refers to themselves and they use a masculine pronoun."],[2462.81,"Now, Von Harnack, of course, said, well, this is because of, you know, patriarchal authority structures in the church, and so she had to conceal her gender and blah blah blah blah blah."],[2472.17,"Which is what we call special pleading"],[2476.37,"In terms of if you've taken an intro to logic class, that is what we call special pleading."],[2482.13,"Um just dismiss casually dismissing the uh the evidence against your point, which uh which that is."],[2490.93,"So and there there is again, there's no positive evidence anywhere or any reason to believe that that a woman wrote it over against a man and was using a fake pronoun that one time."],[2503.02,"Like there's just no logical reason."],[2506.06,"Um so yeah."],[2510.22,"Von Harnack was very much in a mode of uh everything you think you know about the Bible is wrong."],[2516.78,"All traditions are wrong"],[2520.0,"Um surprisingly postmodern for a 19th century German."],[2526.8,"Uh but just sort of the general wisdom must always be wrong."],[2531.04,"And so I think we can safely set that aside."],[2534.4,"It's not impossible."],[2536.42,"Like I can't prove to you that St."],[2538.42,"Priscilla didn't write it, but there's no reason to think she did."],[2545.3,"And a couple of reasons to think that like the pronoun being used refers to that it was uh a male human who wrote it."],[2555.62,"Um so yeah, so those those are kind of the major there, and you'll get some other weird random ones of you know, Timothy or St."],[2564.78,"Timothy or some other associate of St."],[2567.26,"Paul, but they fall into that same category."],[2568.78,"And the ones I just mentioned, St."],[2570.14,"Barnabas, St. Apollos, St."],[2571.9,"Persona, those are the ones that you see all over the place."],[2574.78,"Especially the St."],[2575.5,"Priscilla thing now."],[2576.62,"People are all excited about that."],[2579.18,"Da Vinci Code got them all worked up."],[2582.3,"Uh so that's sort of the authorship thing."],[2588.86,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2596.3,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancientfaith.com."],[2601.58,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.72,"lv":2,"h":"Starting Hebrews: what to expect","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tonight mostly <b>introduction</b> - \"a lot to talk about\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Calls it <b>St. Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews</b>, but</li><li class=\"d1\">many <b>church fathers</b> don't think Paul wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not really an epistle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not sure it was written to a group of <b>Hebrews</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"like the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b> thing in Voltaire\" - neither holy, Roman, nor an empire</li><li class=\"d0\">Start with the <b>St. Paul</b> part</li><li class=\"d1\">title firmly established <b>very early</b>, even among those who doubted Paul wrote it</li></ul>"},{"t":105.68,"lv":2,"h":"How Paul's epistles were written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No single \"original text\" of NT epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>dictated</b>, didn't write by hand</li><li class=\"d1\">clues: \"<b>see, I am writing with my own hand</b>\" / \"what large letters\" - change from scribe's hand</li><li class=\"d0\">Scribe = <i>amanuensis</i></li><li class=\"d1\">writes down dictation, <b>corrects grammar</b>, polishes into literary form - spoken vs written differ</li><li class=\"d1\">gives it back to the author to <b>review and correct</b></li><li class=\"d1\">scribe carries letter to recipients, <b>reads it aloud</b>, answers questions</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Corinthians</b> = contents of at least <b>two</b> epistles edited together</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wrote <b>4-6 epistles</b> to Corinth; breaks still visible</li></ul>"},{"t":267.19,"lv":2,"h":"Collections of Paul's epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Books did <b>not circulate individually</b> for long</li><li class=\"d1\">at first yes - Paul's epistles, Gospels, general epistles, Revelation each written separately</li><li class=\"d1\">quickly <b>gathered into collections</b> - Paul's first</li><li class=\"d0\">All gathered together by <b>100 AD</b>, maybe earlier</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 Peter</b>: refers to \"Paul's <b>epistles</b>\" plural - readers knew more than one, already circulating</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Every manuscript</b> we have of Paul = copy of a <b>collection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a single manuscript of an individual epistle circulating alone</li><li class=\"d1\">in the 2nd c., only as collection</li></ul>"},{"t":389.97,"lv":3,"h":"P46 and the papyrus manuscripts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Earliest manuscript of Paul: <b>P46</b> - P = <b>papyrus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">later ones on <b>vellum</b> (animal skin); papyrus = \"primitive paper\" from bashed reeds</li><li class=\"d1\">found mostly in <b>Egypt</b>, a few in <b>Judean desert</b> (Dead Sea Scrolls) - dry climate; \"not in Louisiana\"</li><li class=\"d0\">46th papyrus biblical manuscript found - numbered in order of discovery</li><li class=\"d0\">Parts of all the books; holes in the pages</li><li class=\"d0\">Dates to <b>second half of 2nd c.</b> (150-200)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Includes Hebrews</b> - in order by length, <b>second longest after Romans</b></li></ul>"},{"t":553.57,"lv":3,"h":"What the manuscripts show about Hebrews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Whoever first gathered and ordered them treated Hebrews <b>like the rest of Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">saw no reason for a different collection</li><li class=\"d0\">From c. <b>150 AD</b> the <b>general (Catholic) epistles</b> circulate together - James, Peter, John, Jude</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hebrews is never in it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Scribes put it in the <b>Paul collection</b>, not the other apostolic collection</li><li class=\"d1\">why it's titled <b>Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also: Hebrews was read <b>authoritatively in churches</b></li></ul>"},{"t":637.11,"lv":2,"h":"Digression: how canon works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Misrepresentations of how they \"decided what's in the Bible\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nobody ever voted</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Nicaea</b> said nothing about the NT canon</li><li class=\"d1\">biggest misnomer: people examining books to decide if canonical</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Canon</b> = works with <b>authority and prominence</b> in a culture or community</li><li class=\"d1\">also used of \"Western canon\" of literature; \"<b>British canon</b>\" of mid-late 20th c. pop music</li><li class=\"d0\">Early Christian communities had texts they read <b>liturgically</b> from the beginning</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul in synagogues: scrolls opened, <b>Hebrew scriptures</b> read, he preaches Christ</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 Peter</b> lumps Paul's epistles with \"the <b>other scriptures</b>\" - mid-60s</li><li class=\"d0\">Is a book canonical? <b>Do we read it in the services?</b> Yes or no</li></ul>"},{"t":818.22,"lv":3,"h":"Different Judaisms, different canons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christianity inherited it from the various <b>Judaisms</b> - each had different books</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Qumran</b> (non-Christian Jewish group, ended 1st c.) - most copies in Dead Sea Scrolls</li><li class=\"d1\">1 <b>Genesis</b>, 2 <b>1 Enoch</b>, 3 <b>Jubilees</b>, 4 <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharisees</b> different; <b>Sadducees</b> accepted only the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Alexandria</b>: 2nd, 3rd <b>Maccabees</b>; <b>Ethiopian Jews</b>: huge volume of literature</li><li class=\"d0\">Christian communities took over their community's OT, then added <b>apostolic writings</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No one argued about which books were canonical</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisees and Sadducees argued <b>resurrection, theology, interpreting the law</b> - not books</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody asked Ethiopian Jews \"why this weird stuff?\" - \"that's their way\"</li></ul>"},{"t":950.4,"lv":3,"h":"Judging books by what was received","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Churches did the same - against <b>Gnostic gospels</b> and heretical literature</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Irenaeus</b>, <i>Against Heresies</i> (2nd c.): attacks <b>teaching</b>, not books</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not descended from the apostles; they teach this, <b>not what the apostles taught</b>\" - \"and they get it from these goofy books\"</li><li class=\"d0\">None are <b>sola scriptura</b> - books a <b>function of belief</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bring <b>Gospel of Truth</b> or <b>Thomas</b> to a community in early 3rd c.: \"that's not what we believe\"</li><li class=\"d1\">judged books by what they <b>received</b>, not vice versa - <b>living memory</b> back to the apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">No debates like \"excommunicate if you don't accept 2 Peter\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1065.18,"lv":3,"h":"Canon grows organically","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Communities grew, persecution abated, they made <b>contact</b>, saw similarities and differences</li><li class=\"d1\">some had no <b>2-3 John</b> - didn't think them heretical, never saw them</li><li class=\"d1\">met a community with them, <b>adopted</b> them - gradual</li><li class=\"d0\">At any time canon is <b>objective</b> - certain texts treated as authoritative in each community</li><li class=\"d1\">no table of contents or list needed</li><li class=\"d0\">First NT list matching ours: <b>St. Athanasius</b>, <b>Paschal letter</b>, <b>387</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one bishop can't do it for the whole church</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"these are the inspired books\" but \"<b>I don't want any others read in my churches</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not telling a church to add 2 Peter - telling them <b>stop reading Gospel of Thomas, Judith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no Old Testament list</li></ul>"},{"t":1205.59,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews in the West and the East","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews <b>unknown in the West until the 4th c.</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not fully embraced - quoted authoritatively, commented on - until the <b>early 5th c.</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mostly <b>Augustine</b> and <b>Jerome</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Seeded digression: why no one in the West talks about <b>atonement</b> before then</li><li class=\"d1\">big chunk of Hebrews = Christ and the <b>Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>East</b>: quoted, commented on, treated as Paul's from the beginning</li><li class=\"d0\">Some books <b>universally accepted early</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>four Gospels</b> by 150 - \"just these four\"</li><li class=\"d1\">amazing: Gnostic gospels very different; compared to <b>Thomas</b>, John and the synoptics \"identical\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no church using just one - all four <b>in tandem from the beginning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Others take forever: <b>Revelation</b> - Western church until 15th c., Eastern until 6th c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews slow in West, not East - theories (Semitic languages in the East?) all <b>conjecture</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1408.52,"lv":2,"h":"Greek style problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">East accepts Hebrews as part of the <b>Pauline corpus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But readers noticed things - is it really an <b>epistle</b>? (later)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Greek and style very different</b> from Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">not the minor differences of the <b>pastorals</b>, <b>Ephesians</b>, <b>Colossians</b> - those had ready explanations (person vs community, different themes)</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>Romans</b> vs <b>Mark's Gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Mark's Greek \"rustic\" - \"terrible\"; powerful things, bad style</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Luke</b> and <b>Paul</b> polished - Paul writes like <b>Epictetus</b>, \"impressive and complicated\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I love Mark's gospel, hate reading it in Greek\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews vs Romans: \"do not look written by the same person\" - beyond a different scribe</li><li class=\"d0\">Yet <b>concepts</b>, use and interpretation of <b>OT quotes</b>, phrasing look very Pauline</li></ul>"},{"t":1600.15,"lv":2,"h":"Patristic theories of authorship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just modern critics - <b>fathers</b> noticed</li><li class=\"d1\">Western fathers used to Paul, meet this new thing: \"what's up?\" - not rejecting it</li><li class=\"d0\">One set of theories: <b>Paul and someone else</b> both involved</li><li class=\"d0\">Variation 1: a close <b>associate</b> wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul had many companions, co-authors esp. <b>Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same theology (mentored by Paul, like <b>Timothy, Titus</b>), different style</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul involved, not directly</li><li class=\"d0\">Variation 2: Paul produced <i>something</i>, an associate turned it into Hebrews</li><li class=\"d1\">long-lived theory - <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> (1200s)</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wrote in <b>Hebrew or Aramaic</b> for Jewish readers; <b>Luke translated</b> into Greek</li></ul>"},{"t":1782.33,"lv":3,"h":"The case for St. Luke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>Luke</b>: many places where Hebrews' Greek <b>looks like Luke's</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Luke's works are <b>narrative</b> - Gospel and Acts, latter part focused on Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">he cites research and sources</li><li class=\"d1\">deliberately imitates <b>Thucydides</b> - Greek <b>historical style</b>, putting works in the genre of history (ancient, not modern)</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews is <b>not historical narrative</b> at all</li><li class=\"d1\">style links to Luke odd if another genre and not the same author</li><li class=\"d1\">\"best evidence for Luke being involved\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Cards on the table\": his <b>personal opinion</b> by end of intro - <b>Luke involved</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a <b>single teaching of the church</b> - fathers and later authors \"all over the place\"</li><li class=\"d1\">connection too strong for another genre; Luke closely associated with Paul, traveled with him</li></ul>"},{"t":1942.56,"lv":2,"h":"Other proposed authors: Barnabas and Apollos","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Other theories: some other Pauline affiliate - fathers and moderns \"going to bat\" for many</li><li class=\"d0\">Oddest one: an author suggested the <b>Theotokos</b> wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">no one else in church history defends that - \"intriguing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">possibly a <b>pseudo</b>- writer, maybe pseudo-Augustine - will check</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tertullian</b> (while still a Christian): <b>Barnabas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">popular pick down to <b>Martin Luther</b> and contemporary scholars</li><li class=\"d1\">argument: Greek similar to the <b>Epistle of Barnabas</b> - presupposes Barnabas wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing by Barnabas in the NT; that epistle never held authority</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apollos</b>: popular, but nothing of his to compare</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: can't argue for <b>this associate over all others</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2143.86,"lv":2,"h":"Priscilla and von Harnack","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Adolf von Harnack</b>, \"king of 19th c. German NT scholarship\" - \"most German name ever\"</li><li class=\"d1\">proposed <b>St. Priscilla</b> (of Priscilla and Aquila) - popular today</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Steel man\" argument</li><li class=\"d1\">general consensus: written to or for the <b>Jewish community in Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Priscilla and Aquila: among the <b>70</b>, saints - \"nothing negative about them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">expelled from Rome under <b>Claudius</b>, to Corinth, met Paul, became Christians; church met in their home; Aquila later bishop</li><li class=\"d1\">Priscilla <b>named first</b> at several points - likely prominent family, wealth, <b>inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">under Torah, <b>eldest daughter inherits</b> if no sons - dispute settled by Moses in <b>Numbers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">von Harnack and recent writers: prominent, from that community - \"so I bet she wrote it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2345.7,"lv":3,"h":"Google and the modern fascination","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Googled \"who wrote Hebrews\" - curious if \"nobody knows\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Origen</b>: \"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews because that's how he received it... who actually wrote it, <b>God knows</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Prominent results: \"<b>a woman wrote a book of the New Testament</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the church has been lying for 2,000 years\" - \"all that nonsense\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Urges: do your own research - he gave all the evidence, no straw man</li></ul>"},{"t":2425.69,"lv":3,"h":"Why Priscilla doesn't hold up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All evidence: Priscilla and Aquila from the community it was written for</li><li class=\"d1\">no reason to pick her over her husband</li><li class=\"d0\">Against: near the end the author uses a <b>masculine pronoun</b> of himself</li><li class=\"d1\">von Harnack: <b>patriarchal structures</b> - she concealed her gender</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>special pleading</b> (intro logic) - casually dismissing counter-evidence</li><li class=\"d1\">no positive evidence for a woman using a fake pronoun once</li><li class=\"d0\">von Harnack: \"everything you think you know about the Bible is wrong\", \"all traditions are wrong\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"surprisingly <b>postmodern</b> for a 19th c. German\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't prove she didn't write it - but no reason to think she did, and a couple to think a <b>male</b> did</li></ul>"},{"t":2555.62,"lv":3,"h":"Wrap-up on authorship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Other random ones: Timothy or another Pauline associate - same category</li><li class=\"d0\">Seen everywhere: <b>Barnabas</b>, <b>Apollos</b>, <b>Priscilla</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Priscilla - \"people are all excited\"; <b>Da Vinci Code</b> got them worked up</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's sort of the authorship thing\"</li></ul>"}]});
